SQL Server showplan XML generated from a query shaped like:

```
INSERT INTO ReadingListBooks (ReadingListId, BookId)
SELECT rl.Id, b.Id FROM ReadingLists rl, Books b
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM ReadingListBooks rlb
    WHERE rlb.ReadingListId = rl.Id AND rlb.BookId = b.Id
)
```

Mirrors the real shape from alloy#6941 (query_hash c1bf3f3945559e7f): the
query optimizer wraps the INSERT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS in an Assert to
enforce the uniqueness check, so Assert sits above the Update rather than
the other way around.

-- assert_operator.xml --
<ShowPlanXML xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/sqlserver/2004/07/showplan">
  <BatchSequence>
    <Batch>
      <Statements>
        <StmtSimple>
          <QueryPlan>
            <RelOp PhysicalOp="Assert" LogicalOp="Assert" EstimateRows="1" EstimatedTotalSubtreeCost="10.75">
              <Assert StartupExpression="0">
                <RelOp PhysicalOp="Clustered Index Insert" LogicalOp="Insert" EstimateRows="1.5" EstimatedTotalSubtreeCost="10.74">
                  <Update>
                    <Object Database="[MyDb]" Schema="[dbo]" Table="[ReadingListBooks]" Index="[PK_ReadingListBooks]" IndexKind="Clustered" />
                    <RelOp PhysicalOp="Table Scan" LogicalOp="Table Scan" EstimateRows="2000" EstimatedTotalSubtreeCost="0.5">
                      <TableScan>
                        <Object Database="[MyDb]" Schema="[dbo]" Table="[ReadingLists]" />
                      </TableScan>
                    </RelOp>
                  </Update>
                </RelOp>
              </Assert>
            </RelOp>
          </QueryPlan>
        </StmtSimple>
      </Statements>
    </Batch>
  </BatchSequence>
</ShowPlanXML>
